okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about the multiplication counting  principle now this is something they  test on the act and it's an easy one to  get right if you recognize it and let's  get right into it so basically what the  multiplication counting principle tells  us it tells us  how to find the total number of outcomes  and you can look at this a couple  different ways i'm going to show you  just kind of a visual way to understand  it and then just a quick way to solve  these problems so say for example you  know you had the choice of one of five  science classes  one of three language classes and one of  two math classes and you're going to  pick one of each right for your schedule  how many different schedules could you  create with one science class one  language class in one math class well  one way to visualize this is to make a  tree diagram so let's just look at the  math classes first so you could either  take you know math class number one or  math class number two right so it's kind  of like a you know fork in the row you  make a decision a tree diagram say you  pick math class number one  then you've got three choices for which  language class you take so maybe spanish  italian  or russian okay same thing here  spanish italian russian right  and then from there you could say well  you've got five choices for a science  class so one two three four five one two  three four five and so on  so if you were to draw this you could  see it could be quite large it would  take a long time to draw that  so a shorter method is just to take each  one of these choices okay each one of  the categories i should say and take the  number of choices in each category and  just multiply them all together so 5  times 3 is 15 times 2 is 30 and if you  were to draw this all out if you were to  count all the ends of these branches you  would end up with 30 different roots  essentially so that's the multiplication  counting principle in a nutshell you  multiply the number of  choices in each category together and  that's going to give you the total  number of outcomes so let's look at this  example here see if you can do it on  your own i think you've got it but we'll  go through it here  you go to a restaurant and have the  option of one of two appetizers right  one of five entrees and one of three  desserts how many meals are possible if  you pick one appetizer one entree and  one dessert  okay so you can see  all we have to do is multiply the two  appetizers times the five entrees times  the three desserts and you can see we're  getting in this case 30 which is just a  coincidence with that initial example  that i gave you so that's all you do is  you multiply them together and that  gives you the total number of outcomes
 

